WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.970
In twenty ten, a Danish team saw something they did not expect in a tray of mud taken from the sea floor: the chemistry a few centimeters down was changing instantly, as if it were talking to the surface.

2
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.100
No molecule could possibly cross that distance at such a speed.

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.230
What was flowing through the mud was not matter, but electricity.

4
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.240
Picture a cylinder of mud taken from Aarhus Bay in Denmark.

5
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:42.130
Lifted from the seafloor without being disturbed, carried to the laboratory inside a glass tube, a dark grey mass with a few centimetres of seawater still standing above it.

6
00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:52.660
Mounted over the tube are microsensors whose tips are thinner than a strand of hair; descending one hundred micrometres at a time, they map the chemistry inside the mud point by point.

7
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.630
This is one of the most routine jobs in seafloor research.

8
00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.120
Everyone already knows how the measurement will come out.

9
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:07.770
At the very top, in a thin layer touching the water, there is oxygen, and it is consumed quickly.

10
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.420
It usually cannot reach deeper than two millimetres.

11
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:20.030
Beneath it an oxygen-free world begins, where bacteria break down organic matter with the help of sulfate and release hydrogen sulfide as a waste product.

12
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.880
That is the source of the rotten-egg smell.

13
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.450
The expected picture is simple: where the oxygen ends, the sulfide should begin.

14
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.300
The two curves should touch somewhere.

15
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.310
In the Aarhus measurements they did not touch.

16
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.640
The oxygen ran out and the sulfide did not appear.

17
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:45.290
In between lay a strange gap, sometimes one centimetre thick, sometimes three, in which neither substance was present.

18
00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:53.740
It was as though an invisible hand inside the mud were catching the sulfide seeping up from below, right in the middle, and destroying it.

19
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.070
The oxygen that could burn it was not there.

20
00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:02.800
Nitrate was not there either, and neither manganese nor iron could carry out work this fast.

21
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:13.010
Rather than dismissing this as a measurement error, Lars Peter Nielsen and his team pursued it, and tried something very simple: they removed the oxygen from the water sitting on top of the tube.

22
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:20.980
If the destruction of sulfide down below depended on the oxygen up above, then with the oxygen gone the sulfide should surge upward.

23
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.190
It did.

24
00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:28.760
And it did so in less than one hour, more than twelve millimetres below the surface.

25
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.610
That is where the real shock lies.

26
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:42.140
Molecules travel through mud by diffusing, and diffusion is slow; across twelve millimetres, a chemical message takes hours, sometimes days, to go and come back.

27
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.870
An answer arriving in under an hour cannot be explained by the transport of molecules.

28
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.880
The second clue was hidden in the acidity.

29
00:02:50.880 --> 00:03:02.530
In that thin oxygenated layer at the surface the pH rose to a sharp peak, meaning the environment was turning distinctly alkaline, while immediately below it there was a trough, a shift toward acidity.

30
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.820
This is not a trace that the ordinary workings of mud would leave behind.

31
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:13.990
But show it to an electrochemist and they would not hesitate for a moment: this is the signature of the two ends of a battery.

32
00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:25.320
At one end a half-reaction that gives up electrons and acidifies its surroundings; at the other end a half-reaction that takes up electrons, consumes protons and turns its surroundings alkaline.

33
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.810
And the distance in between is closed by a current.

34
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:37.420
The mass balance went further still: more than forty per cent of the oxygen consumed by the mud was being fed by electrons conducted from below.

35
00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:43.150
Published in two thousand and ten, this result set off a wave of discomfort in the field.

36
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:46.000
Because there was no cable to be seen.

37
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:58.130
The team had listed the possibilities: pyrite crystals in the mud might have formed a network, dissolved carrier molecules might be shuttling back and forth, or bacterial nanowires might be doing the job.

38
00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.980
None of them explained the centimetre scale.

39
00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:08.630
The bacterial nanowires known at the time were limited to micrometres; a centimetre is tens of thousands of times beyond that.

40
00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:13.240
The answer came two years later, by a surprisingly crude method.

41
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:20.250
The researchers passed a tungsten wire fifty micrometres thick horizontally through an undisturbed cylinder of mud.

42
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.620
Like a wire cutting cheese.

43
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:29.790
The structure of the mud was barely affected, the layers stayed where they were, no obstacle arose to the diffusion of molecules.

44
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.080
Yet the electrochemical fingerprint vanished at once.

45
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.370
The pH peak collapsed and the sulfide marched upward.

46
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.380
The invisible connection had been cut.

47
00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:48.550
What is more, when the cut was shallow the system repaired itself within one day; whatever it was, it was growing back.

48
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:57.800
Then, to show that the conductor was not a lifeless mineral network, they placed a layer of tiny glass beads, which do not conduct electricity, inside the mud.

49
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:03.850
Pyrite crystals would not be expected to cross such a barrier and spontaneously build a new circuit.

50
00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:07.020
The connection was established all the same.

51
00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:15.790
When samples from the same mud were put under a microscope, the layers carrying the current turned out to be full of filamentous bacteria centimetres in length.

52
00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:23.440
Living threads running the whole distance, carrying fine stripes along their bodies, divided internally into compartments.

53
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.970
The cable was alive.

54
00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:31.380
Under a microscope a cable bacterium looks ordinary enough to be overlooked.

55
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.630
Its thickness is between one and four micrometres, about one per cent of a human hair.

56
00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:40.440
But try to follow it to its end and the situation changes.

57
00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:47.290
It leaves your field of view, continues in the neighbouring fields, passes millimetres, reaches centimetres.

58
00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:58.940
Inside it, thousands of cells, sometimes more than ten thousand, are lined up end to end; each is three to five micrometres long, enclosed within a shared outer sheath like the carriages of a train.

59
00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.230
Not a loose queue of individual bacteria, but a single body.

60
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:09.440
The way this body works rests on a division of labour that is hard to match anywhere in the microbial world.

61
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:20.770
The cells at the lower end of the filament, buried in the mud, oxidise hydrogen sulfide; instead of consuming the electrons from that reaction themselves, they hand them to the filament.

62
00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:26.020
That is the negative end of the circuit, and it is what acidifies its surroundings.

63
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:35.910
The cells at the upper end, reaching the surface, reduce oxygen to water; the electrons they need come not from their own neighbourhood but along the line arriving from below.

64
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:41.000
Because they consume protons, the environment there turns alkaline.

65
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:49.450
In other words, the distance between the pH peak and the pH trough in those measurements is in fact the length of a single organism.

66
00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:54.700
To see how contrary this is, it is enough to look at the scales.

67
00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:01.870
A cell normally completes its energy metabolism within its own membrane, across a distance measured in nanometres.

68
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:08.400
The cable bacterium has spread that same task over a distance tens of thousands of times its own width.

69
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:19.730
To put it crudely, it is like leaving your lungs behind and breathing through a snorkel kilometres long; the comparison is of course flawed, because what travels here is not air but the electron itself.

70
00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:24.180
The success of this strange way of life in the mud is anything but modest.

71
00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:36.150
In the laboratory, a community settling into fresh mud colonises the top fifteen millimetres from end to end within the first ten days, and in that early phase the doubling time of the cells is under twenty hours.

72
00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:44.120
On the twenty-first day the density peaks, and beneath every square centimetre of surface a total of two kilometres of filament has accumulated.

73
00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:55.290
Seen from above, those characteristic alkaline patches at the surface first appear as isolated islands, then spread sideways at a rate of zero point three to one point two centimetres per day.

74
00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:00.540
Above the mud, a network invisible to the eye grows at a speed that is visible to the eye.

75
00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.710
And this network is everywhere.

76
00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:12.320
After first being found on the Danish coast, it was found in the Netherlands, in Japan, in Australia, along the coasts of the United States.

77
00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:20.770
The branch living in salt and brackish water is called Electrothrix in scientific naming, and the branch living in fresh water is called Electronema;

78
00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:25.540
from here on I will simply call them the marine branch and the freshwater branch.

79
00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:36.390
The marine branch lives in estuaries, in salt marshes and in seagrass meadows; the freshwater branch lives in streams, on lake bottoms, even in flooded rice paddies.

80
00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.920
Nor are they alone.

81
00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:46.250
Observations published in two thousand and twenty-three showed that these filaments gather a small crowd around themselves in the mud.

82
00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:53.260
Other kinds of bacteria, ones that can swim, collect in a dense cloud around the oxygen-free stretch of the filament.

83
00:08:53.260 --> 00:09:00.110
When researchers cut the filament with a laser and severed its connection to oxygen, the cloud dispersed immediately.

84
00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:08.240
Cells sitting closer to the filament can be measured to be in a chemically more oxidised state, yet physical contact is rare and brief.

85
00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:19.090
The most plausible reading is that these bacteria pass their electrons to the cable through dissolved intermediary molecules; this is not yet a proven mechanism but an explanation waiting to be tested.

86
00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:25.620
Still, the picture is clear: inside the mud there is an electrical line that gathers subscribers around itself.

87
00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.830
One question remains.

88
00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.360
What is this line made of?

89
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:39.130
If you carefully peel away the outer sheath of a cable bacterium filament and put it under an electron microscope, you see parallel ridges running its length.

90
00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:46.940
Bundles of fibres, numbering between fifteen and sixty depending on the species, each roughly fifty nanometres across.

91
00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:56.510
The most crucial property of these fibres is that they do not stop at cell boundaries: they run from one cell into the next, passing uninterrupted through the junction between them.

92
00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:01.760
There are thousands of cells along the filament, but a single unbroken fibre network.

93
00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:05.730
Stranger still, this network does not need to be alive.

94
00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:12.740
Strip the cells away chemically until only this fibre skeleton remains, and the skeleton still conducts electricity.

95
00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:22.310
Measurements give conductivities of up to twenty siemens per centimetre for a single fibre, and in measurements made on intact filaments the value rises as high as seventy-nine.

96
00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:26.440
This is a figure no known biological structure comes close to.

97
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:36.810
For comparison: the estimated value for the nanowires of the genus Shewanella is around zero point zero three siemens per centimetre, that is, nearly one thousand times lower.

98
00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:41.740
Geobacter pili stay between zero point zero five and one point five.

99
00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:51.630
The longest conducting filament tested was ten point one millimetres and spanned more than two thousand cells; the resistance of a single cell could fall as low as five kilo-ohms.

100
00:10:51.630 --> 00:11:02.640
The density of the current passing through the fibres corresponds to about six hundred and fifty thousand amperes per square metre; the density carried by the copper wiring in our homes is only a few times higher.

101
00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.370
An objection comes to mind: perhaps this is the movement of ions rather than electrons.

102
00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:12.340
In watery environments charged particles certainly carry current.

103
00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:17.430
For that reason the fibres were tested in a high vacuum below ten to the minus seven bar.

104
00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:23.000
In an environment without water or ions, the conductivity held steady for weeks.

105
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.170
What was being carried was the electron itself.

106
00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:28.540
That was the real puzzle.

107
00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:38.750
Proteins do transport electrons, yes, but they do it by hopping between iron-bearing stations called cytochromes, and the range of those hops is measured in nanometres.

108
00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:42.720
A centimetre is an astronomical distance for that mechanism.

109
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:55.170
In two thousand and twenty-one, a study carried out with contributions from roughly twenty research centres used Raman microscopy to obtain the chemical fingerprint of the fibres, and found an unexpected metal: nickel.

110
00:11:55.170 --> 00:12:01.220
Bonded to sulfur atoms, moreover, in an arrangement never seen before in biology.

111
00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:12.390
Looked at in cross-section, the structure resembled a household cable: at the centre a nickel-rich conducting core occupying about twenty-seven per cent of the cross-sectional area, wrapped in a nickel-poor,

112
00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.400
insulating protein sheath.

113
00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:21.450
When the nickel was chemically oxidised, or selectively stripped out, the conductivity collapsed.

114
00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:26.220
It cannot be said that nickel is absent from living things.

115
00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:33.550
Enzymes such as urease, some hydrogenases and carbon monoxide dehydrogenase have nickel at their working centre.

116
00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:37.680
But there nickel is a catalyst driving a reaction.

117
00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:45.810
Here it is not what performs the reaction; it is the backbone of a line that carries the electron, not metres but certainly centimetres, away.

118
00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:55.380
This is a previously undescribed mechanism, one that pushes the range of known protein conductivity in biology from the micrometre scale to the centimetre scale.

119
00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:04.150
The metal cable analogy should not be taken too far: there was no nickel wire there; there was a protein arranging nickel atoms in a regular order.

120
00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:15.000
The exact atomic-level architecture of this structure, and the precise path the electron follows along the core, are still disputed; this is not a closed case but an open field of research.

121
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:19.130
The weight of the discovery did not stay confined to the laboratory.

122
00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:27.580
When cable bacteria colonise a patch of mud, they clear the hydrogen sulfide out of the environment and keep cycling the sulfur back into sulfate.

123
00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:40.030
In experiments with pots of rice paddy soil, sulfate increased fivefold in the soils inoculated with these bacteria, and methane emissions fell by ninety-three per cent compared with uninoculated control pots,

124
00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:44.960
because methane-producing microbes lose out in competition with sulfate breathers.

125
00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:53.090
Honesty requires adding that this is a result at the scale of a pot, and that whether the same effect would appear in a real paddy field has not yet been shown.

126
00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:56.100
The family also keeps growing.

127
00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:03.590
In April two thousand and twenty-five, a new species was described from the tidal flats of Yaquina Bay on the coast of Oregon.

128
00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:14.760
Its filaments carry far fewer ridges than its relatives, between ten and thirteen, but noticeably wider ones; its genome combines features of the marine branch and the freshwater branch.

129
00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:26.890
The species was named after the Yaqona people, whose ancestral lands include that bay and river, and the naming was done together with Yaqona descendants who live today under the Confederated Tribes of Siletz Indians.

130
00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:32.460
And here is what really whets the appetite of engineers: nobody manufactures this cable.

131
00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:38.190
It is not mined, it needs no clean room, it is not synthesised at high temperature.

132
00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:48.560
Inside the mud it extends itself at a rate of up to one centimetre per day, repairs itself when cut, and dissolves when its work is done without leaving toxic waste behind.

133
00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:58.930
Whether this will one day lead to self-growing, degradable bioelectronic materials is at present no more than a guess; there is as yet no product, not even a mature prototype.

134
00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:09.940
What is certain is that the silent, foul-smelling layer of mud on the seafloor, the one nobody thought worth looking at, had already found electronics' solution to the centimetre scale long before we did,

135
00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:18.070
within an engineering tradition roughly four billion years old, and all it asked in order to show us was that someone lower a sensor into it slowly enough.

136
00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:22.040
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

137
00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:25.850
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
